WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:01.968
-My first guest is an Emmy

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.135
and Golden Globe-nominated
actress

00:00:03.135 --> 00:00:06.172
who stars alongside Jodie Foster
in the new movie

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:08.174
"The Mauritanian,"
which is in theaters now

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.042
and on demand March 2nd.

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:12.878
Here's one of our favorites,
Shailene Woodley, everybody.

00:00:12.878 --> 00:00:14.881
Hi.
-Hi!

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.516
-It is so nice to see you,
buddy.

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.451
Thanks so much
for coming back to our show.

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:21.754
I appreciate it.
-Yeah, thanks for having me.

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:25.391
-I want to talk to you
about "The Mauritanian."

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:28.160
You're fantastic in it,
but before we get into that,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.229
I have a lot of questions.

00:00:30.229 --> 00:00:33.299
One is that I keep reading

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:37.603
that you are engaged to football
great Aaron Rodgers.

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:41.240
Is that -- Is that true?
Are congratulations in order?

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.875
-I mean,
I've been reading that, too,

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:45.078
which is kind --
it's kind of funny.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.846
-Yeah.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.648
-Yes, we are engaged.
We are engaged.

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:50.483
But for us, it's not new news,
you know?

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:52.185
So it's kind of funny.
Everybody right now

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:53.953
is freaking out over it,
and we're like,

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.755
"Yeah, we've been engaged
for a while."

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.957
[ Chuckles ]
-I love that!

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:03.296
-Yeah, we -- we got engaged
a while ago, and it's been --

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:05.331
He's, first off,
just a wonderful,

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.032
incredible human being,
but I never thought

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:09.635
I'd be engaged to somebody
who threw balls for a living.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:11.270
Like, I never thought
as a little girl, I was like,

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.307
"Yeah, when I grow up, I'm gonna
marry someone who throws balls.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:17.010
Yeah!"
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.978
-But he's really
just so good at it.

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.213
And I'm impressed.

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:22.548
-He really knows
how to throw a ball, yeah.

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:24.950
-He can throw fastballs,
he can throw slowballs,

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.352
high balls, low balls.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:28.288
When I first met him,
I think my dog --

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:30.223
My dog like pulled me aside
and was like,

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:33.726
"If you don't date this dude,
I will disown you as my mother

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:37.363
because the 3 -- like,
the 3 feet that I run with you

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.132
when you throw a ball is nothing
compared to the marathon

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:42.735
I'm able to run with him."
-Oh, I can't even imagine.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:45.171
I mean he's --
Honestly, he's been on the show

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.239
a bunch of times,
so please give him our best.

00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:50.476
He's one of the greatest players
to ever play the game.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:52.779
Have you spent much time
in Green Bay,

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:57.016
and are you a football fan?
-I -- I still have never been

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.752
to a football game,
because we met

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:03.756
during this wacky, wacky time,
and all of the stadiums

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.024
were closed
that he was playing in.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.360
So I have yet to go
to a football game.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.297
Before I met him, I'd never seen
one football game before.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:12.932
I just didn't -- I didn't
really grow up with sports,

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.835
especially American sports.
It was never really on my radar,

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.971
and so all of --
When we met, also, like,

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:21.807
I knew he was a football guy,
but I didn't know,

00:02:21.807 --> 00:02:24.043
like, what kind
of a football guy he was.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.244
-Oh, my gosh.

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:26.612
-And I'm still
constantly learning.

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:28.881
I'm still like, "Oh, wow!"
You know, friends and --

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:30.850
"You got to watch
his YouTube greatest or --"

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.152
And I was like, "I don't --
I don't get it.

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:35.221
He's good, he's great."
-Yeah, he's really good.

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:37.390
-Like, I don't --
I don't understand.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.991
-That's so funny.
-Because I don't know him

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:41.160
as a football guy.
I know him as like the nerd

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.595
who wants to host "Jeopardy!"

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:44.530
You know, like,
that's the dude I know.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.065
-He loves "Jeopardy!"?
I love that.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:49.302
-He just happens to also
be very good at sports.

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:52.205
-Yeah. Well, that's so --
that's so great for you.

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:55.274
I'm so happy for you.
You know, enjoy this time.

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:57.209
But I also heard
about another thing,

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:00.980
that you took a trip
to Patagonia,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.149
actually even
during COVID, right?

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:04.483
Kind of recently?
-I did.

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:08.087
-What brought you there,
and how did you get to do that?

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:11.557
-I went in December,
which was a feat.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:13.626
I mean, the reason I was able
to go there was because

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:17.229
of work permits and whatnot,
and I kind of got in and got out

00:03:17.229 --> 00:03:19.898
before Chile
shut down its borders.

00:03:19.898 --> 00:03:22.602
But the whole thing was wild.
Like, I landed,

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:24.303
and they check your temperature,
and they were like,

00:03:24.303 --> 00:03:25.905
"Oh, you have a fever.
You have to get

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.239
back on a plane to America."
-Wow.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:28.741
-And I was like, "No, no, no!
Like, I just --

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:30.276
I was wearing a turtleneck
on the plane."

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.710
So I cooled off,
and they checked it again,

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.078
and they were like,
"You're fine."

00:03:33.078 --> 00:03:35.481
-How long is the flight?

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.216
-It was four flights
to get there.

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:38.751
-Yeah.
-Because we took --

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.754
I took a plane from Green Bay
and then a plane from Miami

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:45.358
and then a plane from the main
city in Chile to the second --

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:46.792
to a smaller city in Chile.

00:03:46.792 --> 00:03:49.195
Then we got off the plane,
and we drove for a few hours.

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:51.330
Then we got on a boat
and went up a river

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.699
for 45 minutes or something
like that in pouring rain.

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:54.933
-And then they're like,
"You gotta go back to America."

00:03:54.933 --> 00:03:57.670
And you're like, "No, no, no,
hold on a sec."

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.405
-Yeah, it was wild.

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:01.107
I mean, we were
in the middle of --

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:04.043
we were in a community of people
who the only access they have

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:06.579
to their homes is via river.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.180
There's no roads in or out.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:13.686
So if they want to buy anything,
to bring nails to build a shed,

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:18.557
they have to travel by boat
up and down the river.

00:04:18.557 --> 00:04:21.026
I was down there because there's
this company called Karun

00:04:21.026 --> 00:04:23.161
that I have just been
completely blown away by.

00:04:23.161 --> 00:04:28.067
They empower these local people
to become entrepreneurs,

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:31.804
and at the same time,
they collect plastics and metals

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:33.405
and then they turn them
into eyewear.

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:37.042
So, it's the world's first
100% sustainable,

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:40.046
reusable, renewable...
-Wow.

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:42.815
-...eyewear company.
And it's just --

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:44.183
It's beautiful.
And Patagonia, by the way,

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:45.417
is one of the --

00:04:45.417 --> 00:04:46.952
You feel like
you're on the end of the world.

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:52.224
It's so sunny, and the nature is
so well-preserved and pristine.

00:04:52.224 --> 00:04:54.994
It's a magical place,
if you've never been.

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:56.596
-I do want to go.

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:59.198
I'm gonna wait
till COVID's over,

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:00.666
and I'm gonna wait till
the flight is only

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:03.201
like two hours.
-Yeah.

00:05:03.201 --> 00:05:05.437
-If that's ever possible.

00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:08.775
Let's talk about your new movie,
"The Mauritanian."

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:12.278
Can you set up what it's about,
and what drew you to this?

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:15.548
-Yes. So, this is a film
that's based on a true story

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:17.550
about a man
named Mohamedou Salahi,

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:22.721
who was held in Guantanamo Bay
for over a decade

00:05:22.721 --> 00:05:24.890
even though
he was found innocent.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:27.226
He was held in Guantanamo
without a trial.

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:30.629
And it's the story
about the lawyers

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:33.932
on both sides of the equation --
his lawyers as well as

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:37.135
the lawyers
from the United States --

00:05:37.135 --> 00:05:41.140
helping this man find a trial,
go through the trial,

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.577
and then ultimately have him be
released from Guantanamo Bay.

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:46.645
-Whew.
-Light subject.

00:05:46.645 --> 00:05:50.049
Very light subject.
-Yeah, I mean, can you imagine?

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:51.350
I mean, oh, my gosh.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:54.086
What has to go --
Everything involved with that.

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:56.022
-You know, Jodie Foster
is in the film with me,

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.457
and she said the other day,
she was like,

00:05:57.457 --> 00:05:59.658
"We all have a lot to learn from
him," and I completely agree.

00:05:59.658 --> 00:06:01.494
So I hope
when people see this movie,

00:06:01.494 --> 00:06:05.664
they don't just take away
the tragedy of what --

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:08.734
what the circumstances
but also the humanity

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:11.804
behind this man's strength
and his will to survive,

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.006
and not just survive
but survive in, like,

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:16.908
a really beautiful
and profound way.

00:06:16.908 --> 00:06:18.944
-How is Jodie Foster?

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:21.781
She's just the coolest ever,
right?

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:23.715
-I was gonna
say something sarcastic

00:06:23.715 --> 00:06:24.950
and pretend like she wasn't,
but she is.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:26.318
-Yeah.
-She is --

00:06:26.318 --> 00:06:27.519
You just can't.
You can't do that with Jodie.

00:06:27.519 --> 00:06:29.955
-I know, you can't.
Yeah, she's just --

00:06:29.955 --> 00:06:34.227
-I mean, she is.
She's the -- She's the coolest.

00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:36.929
-And a pro.
-She's a pro.

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:40.099
She's -- I learned so much
from being around her.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.702
I mean, I think the thing
that moves me the most

00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:45.871
about Jodie is she just loves
what she does, and she's --

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:49.508
and she does it for what I would
feel like are the right reasons.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:52.678
And she uplifts
every environment that she's in

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:54.680
because she stays --
she remains curious,

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.316
and she asks questions that
I think a lot of people's minds

00:06:57.316 --> 00:07:01.120
wouldn't even dare
to be curious about.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.990
And in that regard, I just,
you know, again, humanity,

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.192
we all have a lot to learn
from Jodie Foster, as well.

00:07:07.192 --> 00:07:09.394
-There's no one cooler.
The whole cast is great.

00:07:09.394 --> 00:07:11.530
I want to show a clip.
Here's Shailene Woodley

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.033
and Jodie Foster
in "The Mauritanian."

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.001
Take a look.

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:17.569
-What's your point?

00:07:17.569 --> 00:07:20.372
-He's guilty!
He's [bleep] guilty!

00:07:20.372 --> 00:07:22.575
-Maybe he is.

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:24.577
He still has a right to counsel.

00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:26.045
-I'm not saying that he doesn't.

00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:28.581
I'm saying that he helped
to kill 3,000 civilians,

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:30.415
and we're doing everything
we can to get him out.

00:07:30.415 --> 00:07:32.351
-We're doing our job.

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.654
-I did bake sales
for his legal fund.

00:07:34.654 --> 00:07:36.856
That's not a part of my job.

00:07:36.856 --> 00:07:39.091
My dad told me I'm not welcome
home for Thanksgiving this year.

00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:43.595
That's not a part of my job.

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:46.465
-Get out.
-What?

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:50.736
-You want turkey and pumpkin pie
with mom and dad and Uncle Joe?

00:07:50.736 --> 00:07:53.706
Go on, get out.
Go home.

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:55.641
You can't win a case...

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:59.011
if you don't believe
your own [bleep]

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:00.546
-I'm not trying to leave.
I'm just --

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:02.748
-Stop [bleep]
wasting my time.

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:04.583
Get out.

00:08:04.583 --> 00:08:06.418
-Shailene Woodley.
"The Mauritanian"

00:08:06.418 --> 00:08:09.689
is in theaters now
and on demand March 2nd.

